From 10263250c89cd978b0a2a4bb30a5cd86d9aebec6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Josh Hoffer <jhoffer@sansorgan.es>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 13:14:53 -0800
Subject: [RFC PATCH net-next] net: usb: add dbcnet, the debug host's end of a
 DbC network link

dbc_net turns a Linux target's xHCI Debug Capability into a raw IP
network interface. This is the other end: a usbnet minidriver for the
debug host, binding the DbC device dbc_net presents, 1d6b:0012, so two
PCs joined by a USB 3 debug cable get an IP link with no device
controller on either side.

- Framing is dbc_net's: a 4-byte header per packet (magic 0xdb5e, BE16
  length), packets forming a byte stream in each direction. The decoder
  keeps its state across URBs, since frames span them, and delivers
  each packet from rx_fixup.
- Transmit batches packets into one URB, up to 32 KiB. A batch goes
  when the stack has nothing more queued and no transfer is in flight.
  Behind a transfer in flight it waits up to 100 us for more, sent
  from an hrtimer as cdc_ncm does. When byte queue limits or usbnet's
  URB count stop the queue mid-burst, the qdisc hands the rest back
  one packet at a time, each without xmit_more; sending on that alone
  degrades to one URB per packet.
- The target's receive TRBs are 64 KiB, so every transfer must end on
  a short packet: FLAG_SEND_ZLP.
- The interface is raw IP, point to point and without ARP, as
  qmi_wwan's raw-IP mode does it.
- usbnet's own ndo_change_mtu refuses an MTU that is a multiple of the
  packet size. That rule does nothing here, since every frame carries a
  header and every transfer ends short, so the driver sets its own.

Written, with this changelog, by Claude Opus 5.5, an AI coding assistant,
together with the dbc_net series it pairs with. Tested only in QEMU, with
an emulation of DbC the assistant also wrote: enumeration and module
autoload by product ID, MTUs up to 9000, ping and iperf3 both ways, cable
unplug and DbC disable while the link is up.

On 7.4-rc3 in that emulation, host-to-target iperf3 at MTU 1500 went
from 225-255 Mbit/s, with 95% of URBs carrying one packet, to
1000-1130 Mbit/s, 19-30 KiB per URB, and ping under that load from
17-21 ms to 2 ms. The tests ran an out-of-tree copy that differs only
in allocating with kzalloc() and in keeping its own byte queue limits
on kernels before 6.19, whose usbnet has none; this patch was
build-tested on 7.4-rc3.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
